The '''Rugta Baliscano''' is a neighborhood in [[Samara]], [[Burawa]] located in [[District 6, Samara]], north of the [[Old Parliament Neighborhood, Samara|Old Parliament Neighborhood]] and the [[Qayn, Samara|Qayn]]. The neighborhood came to prominence in the late 1970s and early 1980s after the [[Camboriú War]] and [[Special Period]]. The neighborhood has a [[Baliscans|Baliscan]] population of 52% today and is still home to cultural events such as the Baliscano Heritage Street Fair and the celebration of [[Revolution Day (Balisca)|Revolution Day]].

The neighborhood is characterized by 4-6 story walk-up flats constructed between 1914-1934.

== History ==
Baliscano people had first emigrated to Burawa during the 1800s as laborers looking for work after the [[Baliscan Civil War]], some had settled in [[Samara]] though often scattered to the periphery of the city. The first Baliscano residents to move into what would become the neighborhood was reported in 1911 as a Baliscano restaurant tied to Ernesto Crus had opened in the vicinity.

The neighborhood until the 1970s was part of the Old Parliament Neighborhood, but slowly became a hotspot for Baliscano expatriates due to chain-migration. In the 1985 Samara had officially recognized the community as a larger initiative of [[Socialist Reapproachment]] during the decade. The Baliscano population at the time had rose from 4% in 1974 to 83% by 1985. 

The 2000s had started to see a decline of Baliscano population as many newly minted citizens of Burawa had chosen to move to newer housing further away from the core districts. Population continued to change as the 2010s had seen a resurgence of interest in older housing styles, causing the neighborhood to have high move-in demand and low housing availability. 

== Demographics ==
In 2020, Baliscan population had constituted 52% of the population, most aged between 50-70 years old. The second largest age group consists of adults 25-40, around 38% of Baliscano heritage.

== Events ===
* '''Baliscano Heritage Street Fair''' - includes foods, markets and educational activities 
* '''Revolution Day Parade and Festival''' - a parade that extends from the [[The Samara Parliament Building|Old Parliament Building]] through the neighborhood
* '''Baliscan Lemonade Day''' - A street festival that celebrates [[Baliscan lemonade]]
* '''Day of the Liberated Festival''' - a street festival that celebrates the [[Day of the Liberated]]

== Landmarks ==
=== Baliscan Volunteers Memorial ===
Near [[Hiidan park, Samara|Hiidan Park]] is a large stone monolith dedicated to the Baliscano volunteer fighters who had fought alongside Burawi Socialists during the [[Domestic War]]. The memorial was erected in 2004 by the Daughters of the Domestic War organization.

=== Hashqa Theater ===
[[Hashqa Theater, Samara|Hashin Theater]] is a {{w|Art Deco}} single screen cineplex located on Parliament Street and 25th avenue. It is one of the 16 remaining single screen theaters in Samara built during the [[Golden age of Burawi Cinema]] in 1925.